.Dd August 23, 2026
.Dt SOWA-PKG.CONF 5
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-pkg.conf
.Nd configuration for the Sowa package manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/sowa/pkg.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This file is data.
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
reads it a line at a time: a setting, an
.Sq = ,
and a value with at most one layer of quotes; anything after a
.Sq #
is a comment.
It is read after the command line has been parsed, and the whole of it is five
settings.
.Pp
Nothing in it is executed.
A key that is not one of the five, a key given twice, a value carrying a
.Sq $ ,
a backquote, a backslash, a quote or whitespace, a value outside its own
setting's grammar, or any control character anywhere in the file, is refused
with the line named \(em and the file is not partly applied.
.Pp
That is not fussiness for its own sake.
Under
.Fl -root Ar dir
the file read is
.Pa Ar dir Ns Pa /etc/sowa/pkg.conf ,
which is what lets one machine manage another root with settings of its own \(em
and that root is an image being assembled, a disk that came from somewhere else,
or a container root somebody handed over, while
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
is running on
.Em this
machine, usually as root.
This file used to be sourced.
Reading it as data is what keeps repairing such a tree from running that tree's
configuration file.
.Bl -tag -width SOWA_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE
.It Ev SOWA_REPO_URL
Where the repository is: an
.Ql http
or
.Ql https
URL of a host, an optional port and a path, and nothing else.
No credentials, no query and no fragment \(em none of them mean anything to a
directory of files, and refusing them keeps a configuration file from steering
.Xr curl 1
somewhere with a string that still looks like a repository URL.
Every request appends
.Pa /name
to it, so a trailing slash is stripped rather than left to produce
.Pa //name ,
which most servers answer and some do not.
There is no default: a file that leaves this empty, or one still carrying the
placeholder URL the image is built with, makes
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
stop and say so rather than fetch from somewhere unintended.
.Pp
Plain
.Ql http
works and is warned about once per fetch.
The signature is what an update trusts, so it does not let anyone on the path
ship code to this machine; what it does let them do is see what this machine
asks for.
.It Ev SOWA_REPO_ARCH
The architecture this system is.
.Cm update
refuses an index offering a package built for anything else, which is the check
that keeps a mirror for another architecture from being installed one package
at a time.
The default is
.Ql x86_64 .
.It Ev SOWA_REPO_KEY
The Ed25519 public key the index must be signed with, as an absolute path.
The default is
.Pa /etc/sowa/keys/sowa-repo.pub .
Its private half never leaves the machine that publishes the repository.
.It Ev SOWA_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE
Whether an index must carry a valid signature.
The default is 1, and leaving it there is the whole of the trust model: the
signature is what makes a compromised or impersonated server unable to ship
code to this machine, since nothing else about a plain HTTPS directory can.
Set to 0 only for a repository you built and cannot sign, and note that
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
then warns on every update.
.Pp
A signature says who wrote an index, not that it is the newest one they wrote.
The serial and expiry inside the signed index answer that second question; see
.Sx REPOSITORY FRESHNESS
in
.Xr sowa-pkg 8 .
.It Ev SOWA_RUN_HOOKS
Whether to take the steps packages declare - stopping a daemon before its
program is removed, restarting one an upgrade has replaced, running the setup a
package needs before it works at all.
The default is 1.
Setting it to 0 makes every install and removal purely a matter of files, which
is what a machine that manages its own services wants; it also means removing a
package leaves its daemon running.
.Pp
.Fl -no-hooks
on the command line is read first and is not undone by a file that leaves hooks
on, so the switch always wins.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/sowa/keys" -compact
.It Pa /etc/sowa/pkg.conf
this file.
It is configuration, so
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
keeps a locally edited copy across an upgrade and writes the packaged version
beside it as
.Pa pkg.conf.tpknew .
.It Pa /etc/sowa/keys
where repository keys live.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A repository of your own, signed with a key of your own:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SOWA_REPO_URL="https://packages.example.net/sowa/x86_64"
SOWA_REPO_ARCH="x86_64"
SOWA_REPO_KEY="/etc/sowa/keys/local-repo.pub"
SOWA_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE=1
SOWA_RUN_HOOKS=1
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Anyone who can write this file decides where the machine gets its packages and
which key those packages have to be signed with, which on the running system is
the same authority as writing
.Pa /bin
directly.
It is
.Pa 0644
root-owned for that reason.
.Pp
What is no longer true is that reading it runs it: a line here is a setting, not
a command.
